Chapter: StillsSpec
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter Stills
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 Stills, which defines three records and no values at all.

 THE FAILURE THIS GUARDS AGAINST IS NOT A WRONG NUMBER, IT IS A CHAPTER THAT
 WILL NOT COMPILE. Stills is pure shape: StillPt, StillGrad and StillPoly, with
 nothing to call and nothing to get wrong arithmetically. What can go wrong is
 the front end -- Codex written with syntax an arm does not accept, or syntax an
 arm accepts that the compiler under test has never seen. A chapter with no
 bindings is never exercised by anything except a chapter that USES it, so on
 its own it is not compiled at all.

 So this file's job is to construct one of each record, with every field named,
 and read every field back. StillGrad has FIFTEEN fields; a record literal that
 wide is the widest thing in this port and the most likely to find a limit.

 THE GRADIENT LIST IS THE OTHER THING WORTH FORCING. `grad` is a LIST OF AT MOST
 ONE because Codex has no optional, so both states are constructible and both
 need to be: an empty list on a solid polygon and a one-element list on a shaded
 one. The empty case is the one a compiler is likelier to mishandle, since it is
 an empty list literal at a record-typed element.

 The values are arbitrary and distinct -- consecutive halves and small integers
 -- so that a field read from the wrong neighbour shows up rather than matching
 by luck. That is the same reason PoseSpec's probe carries no zeros.

 We say:

Section: One Of Each

  pt : StillPt
  pt = StillPt { x = 0.5, y = 1.5 }

  grad : StillGrad
  grad = StillGrad { kind = 2, rgba0 = 16711680, rgba1 = 255, off0 = 0.25, off1 = 0.75, ax = 2.5, ay = 3.5, bx = 4.5, by = 5.5, cx = 6.5, cy = 7.5, ux = 8.5, uy = 9.5, vx = 10.5, vy = 11.5 }

  solid : StillPoly
  solid = StillPoly { color = 9066271, grad = [], pts = [pt, StillPt { x = 12.5, y = 13.5 }] }

  shaded : StillPoly
  shaded = StillPoly { color = 4989733, grad = [grad], pts = [pt] }

Section: Reading Every Field Back

 The eleven Reals of a StillGrad in declaration order, then its four Integers,
 then the polygon fields. Split by type because the graders are.

  grad-reals-got : List Real
  grad-reals-got = [grad.off0, grad.off1, grad.ax, grad.ay, grad.bx, grad.by, grad.cx, grad.cy, grad.ux, grad.uy, grad.vx, grad.vy]

  grad-reals-want : List Real
  grad-reals-want = [0.25, 0.75, 2.5, 3.5, 4.5, 5.5, 6.5, 7.5, 8.5, 9.5, 10.5, 11.5]

  grad-ints-got : List Integer
  grad-ints-got = [grad.kind, grad.rgba0, grad.rgba1]

  grad-ints-want : List Integer
  grad-ints-want = [2, 16711680, 255]

 The polygon: colours, the two gradient-list lengths, and the two point counts.
 The empty grad and the one-element grad are the assertion here.

  poly-ints-got : List Integer
  poly-ints-got = [solid.color, shaded.color, list-length solid.grad, list-length shaded.grad, list-length solid.pts, list-length shaded.pts]

  poly-ints-want : List Integer
  poly-ints-want = [9066271, 4989733, 0, 1, 2, 1]

 Reaching THROUGH the one-element gradient list, which is the access the
 renderer actually performs and the one the empty-list encoding exists to make
 possible.

  reach-got : List Real
  reach-got = [(list-at shaded.grad 0).ax, (list-at shaded.pts 0).x, (list-at solid.pts 1).y]

  reach-want : List Real
  reach-want = [2.5, 0.5, 13.5]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "s-gradr" grad-reals-got grad-reals-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "s-gradi" grad-ints-got grad-ints-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "s-poly " poly-ints-got poly-ints-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "s-reach" reach-got reach-want 0.0)
  end
